A||||3||Homicidal||Tighten a slipping/loose belt?||||1||
Z||000000||Homicidal||05-02-2000||10:49 PM||||I know I have to loosen the alternator bolts but I am unsure of which one and how to adjust it after I do.<P>My Chilton doesn't explain it well enough, so I was wondering if anybody could help me out?||||reg||1
Z||000001||N_Man||05-02-2000||11:09 PM||||well you basicly pull the alternater untill the belt feels tight.  It should move about 1/2 inch when adjusted properly.<P>When I got my car the belt squeeled like mad.  I ended up jacking up the car, taking of the rear wheel and using a screwdriver as a prybar on the alternater.  Everything has worked quietly for a little over a month now. Thats for a V6.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||05-02-2000||11:12 PM||||I don't know what engine you have, but if it's a six, loosen the top bolt slightly, so you're not pinching the alternator in the bracket, and then the lower enough to allow it to slide in the track.  Put it up on jackstands for an easier view, and it will be obvious once you get under there.  Be careful not to overtighten and strip the threads on the lower bolt hole, as the alternator body (with the threads) is soft aluminum.  You'll also see that you need a third hand, and maybe a long prybar to hold the tension on the belt while you tighten the lower bolt.  Tighten the upper bolt last.  You should still have a little give to the belt, or you could eat a water pump bearing quick from the side load.  I could almost kill for an automatic belt tensioner.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Homicidal||05-02-2000||11:39 PM||||Well, I have an 84 SE, 4 cylinder.<P>And I need to tighten the top belt, which is located to the right of the alternator.<P>||||reg||1
